      "id": "BwC9LNpCYWg",
      "type": "video",
      "source": "youtube",
      "title": "Shemini Atzeret: The Joy of Torah | Arizal on Sukkot 5 | Rav Eliyahu Bari",
      "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BwC9LNpCYWg",
      "description": [
        "​Why does the journey from Rosh Hashanah through Sukkot culminate in Shemini Atzeret? Rav Eliyahu Bari explores the Arizal’s teaching on spiritual union, the joy of Torah, and light becoming life-giving influence. \n \nThis is Video 5 of 5, the finale of The Arizal on Sukkot: “Shemini Atzeret: What Was the Entire Journey For?” We bring together the themes of separation, rebuilding, judgment, kindness, and the two seals. Their purpose is more than avoiding an unfavorable judgment. The sequence prepares for the fullness of a relationship with Hashem and for a joy that can continue after the festival. \n \nThe principal source is the ninth discourse on Sukkot in Sha’ar HaKavanot, transmitted by Rabbi Chaim Vital. The class distinguishes building a spiritual configuration from preparing for union and from union itself. We then consider the Talmud’s image of a small meal with the king, the mention of rain in Musaf, and the Arizal’s explanation of Simchat Torah. \n \nBUILDING AND UNION \nRachel’s development unfolds through nesirah, standing face to face, receiving chasadim, and receiving two seals. Yet preparing the capacity for a relationship and realizing it are distinct stages. The particular union of Ze’ir Anpin and Rachel discussed here belongs to Musaf on Shemini Atzeret. We do not collapse the different processes of the month into one event. The term zivug describes spiritual union between transmitting and receiving principles, not a physical action. \n \nWHAT WAS THE PREPARATION FOR? \nAs an analogy, tuning an instrument and playing music are connected but different. The preparation has a purpose beyond itself. Likewise, the class asks what happens when the relationship prepared through the festivals becomes a reality. This everyday illustration helps frame the question; it does not replace the Arizal’s technical explanation. \n \nA SMALL MEAL WITH THE KING \nThe Talmud in Sukkah 55b connects the seventy bulls of Sukkot with the seventy nations and the single bull of Shemini Atzeret with Israel. Its parable describes a king who, after a great feast, wants a small meal with his friend. This independent Talmudic teaching helps us hear the theme of particular closeness at the festival’s conclusion. The lesson keeps that parable distinct from the Arizal’s account while considering how both direct attention to the relationship itself. \n \nWHY GEVUROT REMAIN \nThe ninth discourse describes new gevurot received in the union, distinct from those involved in preparing the vessel on Hoshana Rabbah. They are associated with a more sweetened state. Gevurah is not simply another word for evil: strength, definition, and the ability to form a vessel have a place within a corrected relationship. For our own conduct, closeness can coexist with responsibility, and forgiveness need not erase the weight of a promise. These are ethical reflections on the theme. \n \nFROM LIGHT TO WATER \nAt Musaf on Shemini Atzeret, we begin mentioning rain in prayer. The class distinguishes this mention from the seasonal request for rain, whose starting date is not the same everywhere. Drawing on the Zohar, Sha’ar HaKavanot presents “or, mayim, rakia”: light, water, firmament. These describe stages of influence and formation in the source, not a physical theory of weather. The image invites us to ask how the elevated experience of the festivals can nourish ordinary life. \n \nWHY THE JOY OF TORAH? \nThe Arizal explains Simchat Torah through Ze’ir Anpin, called Torah, and the joy of his union with Rachel. This adds a particular Kabbalistic meaning to the joy of completing and renewing the Torah-reading cycle. In Israel, Simchat Torah is celebrated on Shemini Atzeret; in the Diaspora, on the following day. The lesson preserves this distinction while exploring their shared place at the culmination of the festivals. \n \nTHE ARIZAL’S OWN JOY \nRabbi Chaim Vital describes his teacher singing and dancing with the Torah scroll and joining circuits in different synagogues after Yom Tov ended. He also notes that he was not present for the daytime circuits. We retain the limits of that testimony. The account shows how profound study found expression in the joy of a mitzvah, without adding details the student did not witness. \n \nAFTER THE FESTIVAL \nWhat will remain when the festive setting is gone? Choose one continuation: regular Torah study, an act of help, or gratitude before making a demand. Revisit the series from the embrace after forgiveness through the sukkah, four species, and Hoshana Rabbah. The final question is where this renewed relationship will bear fruit in daily life. \n \nSOURCES \nRabbi Chaim Vital, Sha’ar HaKavanot, Derushei Chag HaSukkot, discourse 9 and the testimony in discourses 1–2; Babylonian Talmud, Sukkah 55b; Zohar II, 167a, cited in discourse 9; Vayikra 23:36. \n \nSubscribe for more Torah classes, share this finale, and comment: what will you carry beyond the festival? \n \n#SheminiAtzeret #SimchatTorah #Arizal​"
      ],
