      "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oqzz9uB2oMQ",
      "description": [
        "If the Tree of Knowledge created mixture, confusion, and inner fragmentation, how do we repair it?\n\nIn the final class of Rav Eliyahu Bari’s 8-part series on the Tree of Knowledge, we arrive at the central question: if good and evil became intertwined inside reality and inside the human being, what is the path back to truth?\n\nThe answer is one of the deepest foundations of Torah life: if the damage is mixture, the repair is clarification. If the sin created עירוב — a mixing of good and evil, truth and distortion, holiness and confusion — then the path of tikkun is בירור, the work of sorting, refining, elevating, and realigning. The human mission is to enter a mixed world, identify the sparks of good hidden within it, separate them from distortion, and reconnect them to Hashem.\n\nThis is the role of mitzvot. Mitzvot are not just rituals or routines. They are tools of alignment. They take a world confused after the sin of Adam HaRishon and begin to restore order. A mitzvah creates clarity inside mixed reality. It places an action, object, desire, word, thought, or resource back into its proper relationship with the will of Hashem.\n\nWhen a person eats with a berachah, he is not only eating. He is taking physical energy and placing it into the service of Hashem. Food can remain appetite, or it can become strength for Torah, chesed, tefillah, family, patience, and avodas Hashem. The berachah clarifies the act and reminds us that the physical world is not separate from holiness.\n\nWhen a person gives tzedakah, he is not only transferring money. He is taking material resources and turning them into chesed. Money can become ego, control, anxiety, or self-protection. Through tzedakah, that same money becomes kindness, compassion, responsibility, and partnership with Hashem’s world.\n\nWhen a person learns Torah, he is not only gaining information. He is aligning the mind with Divine truth. After the Tree of Knowledge, the mind can become mixed. Knowledge can become arrogance. Intelligence can become self-importance. Ideas can become weapons. Torah learning repairs the mind by training it to receive truth with humility.\n\nThis is the process of tikkun: step by step, choice by choice, action by action. We take a world where good and evil are mixed and reveal the good hidden inside it. The repair of the Tree of Knowledge happens in daily life: speech, thought, business, food, family, learning, restraint, generosity, modesty, honesty, humility, and responsibility. Every mitzvah is a small act of reordering the world.\n\nRav Eliyahu Bari explains that this is why the teachings of Ramchal, Nefesh HaChaim, the Arizal, and Tanya are essential here. Ramchal in Derech Hashem explains that man has a role in perfecting himself and creation. Nefesh HaChaim teaches that human action below affects higher worlds. The Arizal describes mitzvot as acts of birur and elevation. Tanya explains that mitzvot refine the world and help make a dwelling for Hashem below.\n\nThese teachings reveal that Jewish action is never merely external. A mitzvah performed in this world carries cosmic meaning. A word of Torah, a berachah, an act of tzedakah, a moment of restraint, a choice to speak truth, forgive, honor Shabbat, or guard one’s eyes or tongue — these are not small private gestures. They are part of the repair of the mixture that entered reality after Gan Eden.\n\nNow the entire series comes together. Wheat taught us that knowledge needs vessels. Wine taught us that experience needs boundaries. Fig taught us that self-awareness needs healing. Etrog taught us that goodness needs alignment. The Zohar taught us that all these layers are one system. The Arizal taught us that the mixture of good and evil is now inside us. And mitzvot teach us how to repair it.\n\nThe story of the Tree of Knowledge is not only about what went wrong long ago. It is about what every human being is here to fix now. We live after the Tree, inside a world of mixed motives, desires, perceptions, emotions, and knowledge. Our task is to bring clarity back into the places where confusion entered.\n\nTorah life is practical because the repair is practical. It is in how we eat, speak, use money, think, treat others, learn, pray, respond when no one sees us, and align ordinary life with Hashem. Every mitzvah clarifies another point of reality. Every act of avodas Hashem separates another spark from confusion.\n\n--- IN THIS VIDEO ---\n\n1. How mitzvot repair the Tree of Knowledge\n2. The meaning of tikkun and birur\n3. Practical takeaway for daily life\n\nThis is Part 8, the final class in Rav Eliyahu Bari’s series. Through Ramchal’s Derech Hashem, Nefesh HaChaim Shaar 1, Pri Etz Chaim, Shaar HaMitzvot, Tanya chapters 36–37, Chazal, the Zohar, and the Arizal, this class explains how mitzvot repair the mixture created by the first sin and guide the human being from confusion back to truth.\n\n#TreeOfKnowledge #RavEliyahuBari #TorahClass #JewishWisdom #GanEden #Mitzvot #Tikkun #Birur"
      ],
