      "id": "0f5LdDlc_8g",
      "type": "video",
      "source": "youtube",
      "title": "Tree of Knowledge 7/8 – The Tree of Knowledge Inside the Soul – Rav Eliyahu Bari",
      "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0f5LdDlc_8g",
      "description": [
        "What if the deepest result of the Tree of Knowledge was not only that the world changed, but that the human soul itself changed?\n\nIn Part 7 of Rav Eliyahu Bari’s 8-part series on the Tree of Knowledge, we enter the teaching of the Arizal and explore what happened inside Adam HaRishon — and inside every human being after him.\n\nIn the previous class, we saw the Zohar’s approach: the four opinions of Chazal are parts of one spiritual system. Wheat, wine, fig, and etrog represent mind, emotion, identity, and perception. The Tree of Knowledge stands where these layers meet, and the first sin affected the entire human structure.\n\nThe Arizal now takes us deeper. The Zohar shows the system, but the Arizal asks what that system did to the soul. According to the Arizal in Etz Chaim and related teachings, before the sin, good and evil were not experienced as they are now. They were separate. The human being lived with greater clarity. Truth did not have to be excavated from confusion. Reality was more transparent, and the soul was more aligned.\n\nAfter the sin, everything changed. Good and evil became mixed inside the human being. The struggle moved inward. It was no longer simply a choice between external good and external evil. The chooser himself became mixed. Man now contains clarity and confusion, sincerity and distortion, longing for holiness and attraction to what damages him.\n\nThis explains why human life feels so difficult. A person can want good and still feel pulled elsewhere, have sincere intentions and still misunderstand himself, feel something deeply and still be wrong. Very often, we are drawn to a mixture. We sense something real inside it, but do not know how to separate the good from the brokenness around it.\n\nThis is the Arizal’s language of birur — clarification, sorting, and separating. If good and evil are mixed, then the work of life is to separate them. This sorting happens inside the mind, heart, desire, identity, and perception. Life after Gan Eden becomes a process of birur: identifying the good, extracting it from confusion, rejecting distortion, and returning the spark to holiness.\n\nNow we can reread the four fruits more deeply. Wheat means the mind became mixed: thought can contain wisdom and arrogance, insight and confusion, Torah and ego. Wine means emotion became mixed: inspiration may contain desire, escape, or self-intoxication. Fig means identity became mixed: self-awareness can lead to humility and teshuvah, but also shame and hiding. Etrog means perception became mixed: something may look beautiful, spiritual, and elevated, but still be misaligned with ratzon Hashem.\n\nThis is why moral struggle feels so personal. The battlefield is within us: in our reasoning, emotions, self-image, desires, and perception of what is good. We often stand inside a fog, trying to understand which part of a thought is truth and which part is ego, which part of a feeling is holiness and which part is impulse, which part of shame is humility and which part is despair.\n\nRav Eliyahu Bari explains that the Arizal’s teaching is not meant to create despair. It gives us a map. If the problem is mixture, the solution is birur. If the soul became confused, the work of life is to restore clarity. Through mitzvot, Torah, tefillah, self-honesty, and avodas Hashem, a person separates clarity from confusion and elevates sparks trapped inside the mixture.\n\nThis is why mitzvot are so central in the writings of the Arizal, including Pri Etz Chaim, Shaar HaMitzvot, and related teachings. Mitzvot are acts of birur. They elevate sparks and reconnect fragments of reality to their Divine root. When a person eats with kedushah, speaks with restraint, learns Torah with humility, gives tzedakah, keeps Shabbat, or performs a mitzvah with intention, he is helping repair the mixture created after Adam’s sin.\n\nThe Tree of Knowledge becomes the root of all inner work. Before the sin, good and evil were separate. After the sin, they became mixed within the human being. Every act of growth now requires discernment. Every step toward Hashem requires clarification. Every sincere person must ask: what is the good here, and what is the distortion? What is holy desire, and what is ego? What is truth, and what is merely attractive? What is a spark to elevate, and what is a shell to leave behind?\n\n--- IN THIS VIDEO ---\n\n1. What changed inside the human soul\n2. The Arizal’s teaching on birur\n3. How Torah and mitzvot repair the mixture\n\nThis is Part 7 of Rav Eliyahu Bari’s 8-part series on the Tree of Knowledge, focusing on the Arizal’s teaching about what changed inside the soul after Adam’s sin. Through Etz Chaim, Shaar HaGilgulim, Pri Etz Chaim, Shaar HaMitzvot, and Bereishit 3, this class explores good and evil, birur, the inner battlefield, and the repair of the soul.\n\n#TreeOfKnowledge #RavEliyahuBari #Arizal #TorahClass #JewishWisdom #GanEden #AdamHaRishon #Birur #TorahLearning #JewishThought"
      ],
