      "id": "I0tjxIRjfzg",
      "type": "video",
      "source": "youtube",
      "title": "Tree of Knowledge 5/8 – Four Fruits, One Tree of Knowledge – Rav Eliyahu Bari",
      "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=I0tjxIRjfzg",
      "description": [
        "What if the four opinions of Chazal about the Tree of Knowledge are not competing answers, but four layers of one truth?\n\nIn Part 5 of Rav Eliyahu Bari’s 8-part series on the Tree of Knowledge, we step back from wheat, wine, fig, and etrog to see how these four fruits form a complete spiritual map of the human being.\n\nUntil now, each class focused on one opinion. Wheat revealed the intellectual layer of the sin. Wine revealed the experiential and emotional layer. Fig revealed the psychological layer of shame and self-awareness. Etrog revealed the perceptual layer, where a person confuses what appears good with what is truly right.\n\nWhich one is correct? Was the Tree wheat, wine, fig, or etrog?\n\nThe answer is that all of them are true. They are mapping the human condition. Each opinion reveals another dimension of what happened when Adam and Chava ate from the Tree of Knowledge. The sin was a spiritual rupture that affected the mind, heart, self, and perception.\n\nWheat represents da’at, structured awareness, and the danger of knowledge before its proper time. As Chazal teach in Berachot 40a, a child does not know how to call out to father and mother until he tastes grain. Wheat symbolizes organized consciousness: the ability to name reality and understand relationships. But when knowledge comes before the vessel is ready, it destabilizes.\n\nWine represents experience, emotional intensity, and revelation without containment. In Sanhedrin 70a, Chazal identify the Tree with the vine. Wine loosens boundaries, reveals hidden things, and brings the inner world to the surface. It can be used for Kiddush and simcha, but it can also lead to exposure and downfall. Wine teaches that intensity is not the same as truth.\n\nFig represents self-awareness, shame, and fragmented identity. After the sin, Bereishit 3:7 says that Adam and Chava’s eyes were opened, they knew they were naked, and they sewed fig leaves together. According to the opinion that the Tree was the fig, the same thing through which they fell became the first covering of the fall. The fig reveals the divided self: the person who sees himself, judges himself, compares himself, and feels exposed.\n\nEtrog represents perception, beauty, and the danger of misplaced good. Some sources, including Targum Yonatan to Bereishit 3:6, identify the Tree with the etrog. The etrog is beautiful, fragrant, refined, and connected to mitzvah through “pri etz hadar” in Vayikra 23:40. This teaches that the first sin may have appeared beautiful, wise, and elevated. The danger was mistaking what looked good for what was aligned with ratzon Hashem.\n\nTogether, these four opinions reveal that the Tree of Knowledge was not only about one act of eating. It was a cascade. The mind was affected through premature knowledge. The heart was affected through emotional overflow. The self was affected through shame and fragmentation. Perception was affected through the confusion between appearance and truth.\n\nEvery person lives within these four layers. We struggle with what we know, what we feel, how we see ourselves, and how we decide what is truly good. A person may know something but not be ready to hold it. A person may feel something intensely, but that feeling may not be truth. A person may become self-aware, but that awareness may turn into shame. A person may see something beautiful and assume it is right, even when it is not aligned with Hashem’s will.\n\nThis helps explain why the Torah calls it the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil — עץ הדעת טוב ורע. The problem is mixed knowledge, confused knowledge, and knowledge that no longer arrives with perfect clarity. After the sin, the human being must sort reality: Is this wisdom or arrogance? Is this feeling or truth? Is this shame or humility? Is this goodness, or only the appearance of goodness?\n\nIf we only had wheat, we might think the sin was intellectual. If we only had wine, we might think it was emotional. If we only had fig, we might think it was psychological. If we only had etrog, we might think it was perceptual. But Chazal give us all four because the Tree affected the whole human being.\n\nRav Eliyahu Bari explains that sometimes one subject is described through several opinions because each view reveals another layer of the same truth. Wheat, wine, fig, and etrog are four entrances into da’at tov v’ra, the mixed awareness that defines human life after Gan Eden.\n\n--- IN THIS VIDEO ---\n\n1. Why all four opinions may be true\n2. How wheat, wine, fig, and etrog map the human soul\n3. The deeper meaning of da’at tov v’ra\n\nThis is Part 5 of Rav Eliyahu Bari’s 8-part series on the Tree of Knowledge, bringing together the opinions of wheat, wine, fig, and etrog through Berachot 40a, Sanhedrin 70a, Bereishit Rabbah 15:7 and 19:5, Bereishit 3:6–7, and Targum Yonatan to Bereishit 3:6.\n\n#TreeOfKnowledge #RavEliyahuBari #TorahClass #JewishWisdom #GanEden #AdamAndChava #Chazal #JewishThought #TorahLearning #Daas"
      ],
