      "id": "52rwfWoYfqI",
      "type": "video",
      "source": "youtube",
      "title": "Tree of Knowledge 4/8 – Was the Tree of Knowledge an Etrog? – Rav Eliyahu Bari",
      "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=52rwfWoYfqI",
      "description": [
        "What if the Tree of Knowledge was not ugly, dark, or obviously sinful, but beautiful, refined, and spiritually compelling?\n\nIn Part 4 of Rav Eliyahu Bari's 8-part series on the Tree of Knowledge, we explore the opinion that the Tree was the etrog — and discover one of the deepest tests in avodas Hashem: confusing what appears good with what is truly right.\n\nThe first three parts of this series opened three dimensions of the first sin. Wheat represents premature knowledge — da'at before the soul is ready to contain it. Wine represents overwhelming experience — spiritual intensity without proper vessels. The fig represents self-awareness, shame, and the fractured way a person begins to see himself after the sin.\n\nBut the fourth opinion, identifying the Tree of Knowledge with the etrog, enters an even subtler area: perception itself.\n\nThe etrog is beautiful, fragrant, complete, and refined. It is connected to mitzvah, held on Sukkot as part of the arba minim, and associated with \"pri etz hadar\" in Vayikra 23:40. Some traditions, including Targum Yonatan to Bereishit 3:6, identify the fruit of the Tree of Knowledge with the etrog, placing this beautiful fruit at the center of the first human failure.\n\nThat is what makes this opinion so powerful. The sin did not necessarily begin with something that looked evil. It may have begun with something that looked completely good. Chava saw that the tree was good for eating, desirable to the eyes, and pleasant for gaining wisdom. The problem was not that it seemed destructive — it was that appearance became the measure of truth.\n\nThis is the danger of the etrog: misplaced good.\n\nA person can choose something good in essence, but wrong in timing, context, intention, or alignment with the will of Hashem. Because the thing itself looks elevated, the mistake becomes harder to detect. It is easy to reject darkness when it appears as darkness. It is far more difficult to reject something that looks holy, sounds profound, feels inspiring — but is not what Hashem wants from you now.\n\nThe etrog teaches that even good needs boundaries. Even beauty needs command. Even spiritual desire needs submission to ratzon Hashem.\n\nIn avodas Hashem, this lesson is extremely relevant. A person can pursue something because it looks spiritual or feels holy — but feeling holy is not the same as being aligned with Hashem's will. The Tree of Knowledge, according to this opinion, exposes the danger of allowing beauty, emotion, and appearance to define what is right.\n\nRav Eliyahu Bari explains that this is not only a story about Adam and Chava. It is the story of every person who must learn to distinguish between spiritual appearance and spiritual truth. Sometimes the yetzer hara does not tempt a person with obvious rebellion. Sometimes it dresses the wrong choice in the clothing of beauty, growth, wisdom, and even holiness.\n\nBereishit 3:6 is central because the Torah describes the tree through perception: good for eating, desirable to the eyes, and pleasant for wisdom. But Torah teaches that truth is not defined by appearance. Holiness is measured by relationship to Hashem, obedience, humility, and proper order.\n\nThe etrog therefore reveals a fourth fracture caused by the Tree of Knowledge. Wheat is the fracture of knowledge before its time. Wine is the fracture of experience without vessels. Fig is the fracture of self-consciousness and shame. Etrog is the fracture of perception — confusing what looks good with what is truly right.\n\nAnd yet, the etrog also points toward repair. On Sukkot, the etrog is not taken alone. It is held together with the lulav, hadasim, and aravot, as part of a mitzvah. Its beauty becomes holy when placed inside command, structure, and Divine service. Beauty outside the will of Hashem can mislead; beauty inside the will of Hashem can elevate.\n\n📌 IN THIS VIDEO\n• Why this opinion appears specifically in this parsha\n• How Chazal explain the deeper meaning of the etrog\n• The danger of misplaced good and spiritual appearance\n• How the etrog teaches alignment with ratzon Hashem\n• Practical takeaway for daily avodas Hashem\n\nThis is Part 4 of Rav Eliyahu Bari's 8-part series on the Tree of Knowledge, focusing on the opinion that the Tree was the etrog. Through Targum Yonatan to Bereishit 3:6, Bereishit 3:6, Vayikra 23:40, and Bereishit Rabbah 15:7, this class opens a deeper understanding of Adam and Chava, spiritual perception, misplaced good, and alignment with ratzon Hashem.\n\nIf this teaching gives you a deeper perspective on Gan Eden, the Tree of Knowledge, and the etrog:\n👍 Like the video\n🔔 Subscribe and hit the bell\n💬 Comment with your thoughts\n🔄 Share with others who learn Torah\n\n#TreeOfKnowledge #RavEliyahuBari #TorahClass #JewishWisdom #GanEden #AdamAndChava #Etrog #Chazal #TorahLearning #JewishThought #Kabbalah #InnerTorah #JewishMysticism #AvodasHashem #RatzonHashem"
      ],
