      "title": "Tree of Knowledge 2/8 – Did Wine Cause the First Sin? – Rav Eliyahu Bari",
      "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZAI29UtzuLg",
      "description": [
        "What if the Tree of Knowledge was not only about forbidden knowledge, but about overwhelming experience? In Part 2 of Rav Eliyahu Bari’s 8-part series on the Tree of Knowledge, we explore the astonishing opinion of Chazal that the Tree was a vine — and that wine played a central role in the first human sin.\n\nIn the previous class, Rav Eliyahu Bari explained the opinion that the Tree of Knowledge was wheat. Wheat represents process, structure, development, and da’at that must be prepared step by step. But now Chazal give us a very different image. In Sanhedrin 70a, one opinion teaches that the Tree from which Adam HaRishon ate was a vine. According to this view, the Tree of Knowledge was connected to wine.\n\nThis changes the entire atmosphere of Gan Eden. Wheat represents structured awareness. Wine represents emotional experience. Wheat develops slowly; wine enters directly. It bypasses the ordinary intellectual process and reaches the emotional center of the person. Wine loosens barriers, reveals hidden layers, and uncovers what was concealed beneath the surface.\n\nThis is why Chazal say in Eruvin 65a: “When wine enters, secrets come out.” Wine does not create something new from nothing. It exposes what is already inside. It can reveal joy, love, pain, desire, confusion, vulnerability, and truth. That is why wine is so powerful in Torah. It can be used for Kiddush, simcha, and sanctification, but it can also lead to exposure, downfall, and loss of boundaries.\n\nAccording to this opinion, the sin of Adam and Chava was not only that they knew too soon. It was that they felt too much. The Tree of Knowledge was not merely premature intellect; it was uncontained experience. Adam and Chava were exposed to an intensity they were not yet prepared to hold. The experience entered the heart, imagination, body, and desire before the human vessel had the discipline and structure needed to contain it.\n\nThis is a different kind of danger. Knowledge can destabilize the mind, but experience can overwhelm the whole person. A person may hear an idea and remain composed. But when something is deeply felt, emotional, sensory, exciting, and intoxicating, it can change a person instantly. Wine teaches that not every powerful experience is automatically holy. Intensity can open the soul, but it can also flood it.\n\nThe Torah shows this pattern again after the Flood. Noach leaves the teivah, plants a vineyard, drinks wine, and becomes exposed. In Bereishit 9:20–24, wine is connected to revelation without boundaries. Noach survives the destruction of the world, begins again, and immediately the vineyard appears as a test of human vulnerability. Wine reveals, but if the person is not ready, revelation becomes exposure.\n\nThat is one of the deepest lessons of the Tree of Knowledge. The problem is not wine itself. Wine is not evil. Wine is used in mitzvot, celebration, Shabbat, Yom Tov, weddings, and moments of holiness. The problem is wine without vessels. The problem is intensity without preparation. A person can crave inspiration, spiritual emotion, mystical feeling, and powerful moments of connection, but without Torah, humility, restraint, and maturity, those experiences may not elevate him. They may simply intoxicate him.\n\nThis class is especially relevant in a generation that often confuses intensity with truth. If something feels powerful, many people assume it must be authentic. If something moves them emotionally, they assume it must be holy. But Chazal teach us a more careful approach. A strong feeling is not the same as spiritual truth. Emotional force is not the same as inner repair. Fire can warm, but fire can also burn.\n\nRav Eliyahu Bari explains that according to this opinion, the Tree of Knowledge was the vine because the first sin was the attempt to experience more than the human being was ready to contain. Adam and Chava reached for expansion, sensation, revelation, and emotional intensity before their inner world was properly aligned. The result was not elevation, but fracture; not clarity, but exposure; not sanctified depth, but overflow.\n\n--- IN THIS VIDEO ---\n\n1. Why this idea appears specifically in this parsha\n2. How Chazal explain the deeper meaning of wine\n3. Practical takeaway for daily life\n\nThis is Part 2 of Rav Eliyahu Bari’s 8-part series on the Tree of Knowledge, focusing on the opinion of Chazal that the Tree was wine. Through Sanhedrin 70a, Bereishit Rabbah 19:5, Eruvin 65a, and the story of Noach, this class opens a deeper understanding of desire, spiritual experience, emotional overflow, and the need for vessels in Torah growth.\n\nIf this teaching gives you a deeper perspective on Gan Eden, the Tree of Knowledge, wine, desire, and spiritual maturity, please like the video, subscribe, comment, and share.\n\n#TreeOfKnowledge #RavEliyahuBari #TorahClass #JewishWisdom #GanEden #AdamAndChava #Chazal #WineInTorah #TorahLearning #JewishThought"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "torah",
        "rbari"
      ],
      "date": "2026-05-03"
