      "id": "4S1BtaLbq_s",
      "type": "video",
      "source": "youtube",
      "title": "Ohr HaChaim – Emor – The Kohen’s Daughter and the Soul – Rav Eliyahu Bari",
      "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4S1BtaLbq_s",
      "description": [
        "In this English Torah class on Ohr HaChaim HaKadosh, Parshat Emor, Rav Eliyahu Bari  explains the deep remez hidden in the verses about who may eat from the holy foods. What appears at first to be a law about Kohanim, outsiders, servants, and a Kohen’s daughter becomes a powerful teaching about the soul, spiritual nourishment, sin, holiness, and the heavenly kodesh that sustains the Jewish people.\nOhr HaChaim begins by showing that the word “holy” has two layers. On the level of peshat, it refers to the sanctified foods of the sacrifices, which may not be eaten by a non-Kohen. But on the level of remez, kodesh refers to a higher spiritual sustenance from Heaven. This holy bounty is not merely food; it is the divine life-force that nourishes the soul according to its level, purity, and connection to Hashem.\nThe verse says that an “outsider” may not eat of the holy. Ohr HaChaim reads this as a spiritual principle: not every force, not every desire, and not every inner state can receive the nourishment called kodesh. Holy sustenance must be protected from the forces of impurity. If evil is attached to the soul, giving that soul access to kodesh would also strengthen the outsider clinging to it. Therefore, kodesh is reserved for those whose essence is goodness and whose inner life is aligned with holiness.\nRav Eliyahu Bari explains how Ohr HaChaim reads the Torah’s terms as allusions to the levels of the soul. The neshamah is compared to a Kohen because it is the highest of the three main levels: nefesh, ruach, and neshamah. The ruach is called the “seat” of the Kohen, because the neshamah rests upon the ruach. The nefesh is compared to a hired hand, because it activates human action, movement, effort, and practical life in this world.\nAt first, nefesh and ruach are not naturally fit to receive the lofty sustenance called kodesh. But when the neshamah elevates them through upright deeds, Torah study, mitzvot, and spiritual effort, even the nefesh can rise and become worthy of eating from the holy. This is one of the central ideas of the class: the lower parts of the soul can be lifted. A person is not trapped forever on the level where he begins. Through Torah and mitzvot, the neshamah can acquire the nefesh, refine it, and raise it to a higher spiritual state.\nOhr HaChaim then explains the phrase “someone born in his home.” This refers to holy sparks and souls that fell into the forces of impurity from ancient times, connected to Creation and Adam’s sin. The righteous, through their good deeds, can extract these sparks from the domain of evil. A mitzvah done by a righteous person acts like a magnet, drawing a spark of holiness out from its impure surroundings. These rescued sparks become like “offspring” of the neshamah that elevated them, and they too can rise to receive holy sustenance.\nThe class then turns to the Kohen’s daughter who marries an outsider. Ohr HaChaim explains this as a neshamah that has become blemished through its deeds and attached itself to the outsider, meaning the force of evil. When the soul follows the pleasures of impurity, it loses access to heavenly kodesh. Its spiritual nourishment is reduced, its radiance is dimmed, and its dignity is diminished. This is not only punishment; it is the natural result of spiritual distance from holiness.\nThe final verse speaks about the Kohen’s daughter who becomes a widow or divorcee and returns to her father’s house. Ohr HaChaim reads this as an allusion to the neshamah’s neshamah, the highest soul-level discussed here. Evil cannot truly cling to it. Instead, when sin approaches, it detaches and leaves. This is hinted to by the cantillation mark called tilsha, meaning detached. The higher soul returns to its holy Source, unharmed by the sin itself, but without the earned spiritual independence it could have gained through mitzvot and good deeds.\nThis teaches a powerful lesson about free will and spiritual responsibility. A person can host a lofty soul, but if he violates Hashem’s word, he drives that holiness away. If he fails to produce spiritual fruit in this world, the soul returns to Heaven and must eat from the Father’s table as before, without the dignity of earned reward. By contrast, a soul elevated through Torah and mitzvot enjoys the fruit of its own labor.\n--- IN THIS VIDEO ---\nWhy these verses about holy food reveal the structure of the soul\nHow Ohr HaChaim explains kodesh, nefesh, ruach, and neshamah\nWhat this teaches about sin, mitzvot, holy sparks, and spiritual reward\nIf this class helped you understand Ohr HaChaim, Parshat Emor, and the deeper meaning of the soul’s relationship to holiness, please like, subscribe, comment, and share this Torah teaching with others who want serious study of Chumash, remez, and Jewish spirituality. #OhrHaChaim #ParshatEmor #RavEliyahuBari #Torah #Chumash #JewishLearning #NefeshRuachNeshamah #Kabbalah #Mitzvot #Judaism"
      ],
      "tags": [
        "torah",
        "rbari"
      ],
      "date": "2026-05-01"
